      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND CONTEXT",
          "content": "Operative treatment of symptomatic spondylolysis is rare. A variety of surgeries have been described. In general, reported operative success rates are high, although in most studies the successful return to sport of the competitive athlete is not detailed."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "PURPOSE",
          "content": "To review outcome, specifically return to sport, in a group of competitive athletes after direct pars repair for symptomatic spondylolysis."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "STUDY DESIGN/SETTING",
          "content": "This was a retrospective case series of a single surgeon."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "PATIENT SAMPLE",
          "content": "Series of four athletes who underwent direct pars repair over an 18-month period."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "OUTCOME MEASURES",
          "content": "Return to their sport."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Chart review of all patients who had undergone a single-level pars repair over an 18-month period. All patients had normal discs above the level of the repair. Postoperative clinical course was determined for all patients."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "The patients participated in different sports. All were able to return to their presymptomatic level of activity with no restriction. One had periodic low back pain that required nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory medicine as needed. The others were entirely asymptomatic."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "This small series suggests that with specific inclusion criteria, a select group of motivated athletes can expect to return to their sport after direct pars repairs for symptomatic spondylolysis that has failed conservative treatment."
        }
      ]},
